This project was AGPL-3.0-or-later until August 2026.

Recorded here rather than removed, because a licence change is the kind of fact
somebody needs to be able to find afterwards.

Every release made under AGPL-3.0-or-later remains available under those terms.
That grant cannot be revoked and this is not an attempt to revoke it: anyone
holding a copy of a previous release keeps every right it gave them, in
perpetuity, including the right to fork it and continue under those terms.

The change was possible because the copyright was not distributed. Every commit
to that point was authored either by the sole maintainer or by Dependabot
bumping a version string in a workflow file, so no third party's permission was
required. That is a property of the history and is checkable:

    git log --format='%ae' | sort -u

Why the change. AGPL was chosen to stop a closed fork being hosted at scale with
its improvements kept private, and the reasoning was sound for the software this
was when it was written — a CMS somebody runs. It became the wrong instrument
when the valuable part turned out to be the properties rather than the product:
a template language that cannot execute, a gate that refuses to publish an
unmarked AI-generated page, an accessibility check that blocks rather than
warns. Those are worth more the more places they exist, and reciprocity that a
legal review declines at the first paragraph keeps them in exactly one place.

Apache-2.0 rather than MIT or BSD for the explicit patent grant in section 3.

Contributions are taken under the Developer Certificate of Origin. Copyright
stays with each contributor; there is no contributor licence agreement and no
copyright assignment.
